1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:05,578
The brush uncovers a stone with numbers on it, and one of them is a small circle.

2
00:00:05,578 --> 00:00:08,958
That circle is the most useful thing in this episode.

3
00:00:12,333 --> 00:00:19,555
It is a symbol for nothing — and without it, no column can be empty, and without empty columns, place value does not work.

4
00:00:19,555 --> 00:00:23,613
Somebody had to decide that nothing deserved a sign of its own.

5
00:00:26,300 --> 00:00:28,113
Texts, copied for centuries.

6
00:00:28,113 --> 00:00:32,178
And inscriptions, which are crucial here because they are dated:

7
00:00:32,178 --> 00:00:40,106
a numeral carved on a dated stone proves the system was in ordinary use at that time, not just in a scholar's manuscript.

8
00:00:44,867 --> 00:00:52,272
Indian mathematics begins earlier than most people expect, with the Shulba Sutras, texts on the geometry of altar construction.

9
00:00:55,467 --> 00:01:02,532
Building an altar of a precise area and shape forced practical geometry: converting a square to a circle of the same area,

10
00:01:02,532 --> 00:01:08,506
doubling a square, and relations between the sides of right-angled triangles that are set out as rules.

11
00:01:08,506 --> 00:01:12,885
This is applied mathematics, done because a ritual required exactness.

12
00:01:18,300 --> 00:01:19,629
Then the system itself.

13
00:01:19,629 --> 00:01:24,638
Nine digits whose meaning depends on their column, and a symbol for an empty column.

14
00:01:28,333 --> 00:01:38,666
Aryabhata, born 476 CE, works with it; Brahmagupta, in the seventh century, writes down rules for arithmetic with zero and with negative numbers — including,

15
00:01:38,666 --> 00:01:45,664
correctly, that a number divided by itself is one, and, less successfully, an attempt at division by zero.

16
00:01:45,664 --> 00:01:52,765
The system travels to the Arab world, where scholars develop it further, and reaches Europe from there.

17
00:01:57,500 --> 00:01:58,231
Astronomy.

18
00:01:58,231 --> 00:02:02,680
Aryabhata explains eclipses as shadows and says the earth rotates.

19
00:02:02,680 --> 00:02:09,555
Varahamihira compiles earlier schools, including Greek ones, which is itself evidence of exchange.

20
00:02:14,367 --> 00:02:18,715
Bhaskara the Second, in the twelfth century, writes the Siddhanta Shiromani,

21
00:02:18,715 --> 00:02:25,904
works on algebra and on the motion of planets, and is the last great figure of this tradition before the medieval period.

22
00:02:28,100 --> 00:02:28,933
And medicine.

23
00:02:28,933 --> 00:02:33,333
The Charaka Samhita sets out diagnosis, causes of disease, and treatment,

24
00:02:33,333 --> 00:02:37,379
with an emphasis on prevention and on the physician's conduct.

25
00:02:39,600 --> 00:02:44,956
The Sushruta Samhita describes surgical procedures and lists a large number of instruments,

26
00:02:44,956 --> 00:02:52,116
including operations for cataract and for the reconstruction of a nose — procedures that genuinely startled later readers.

27
00:02:52,116 --> 00:02:57,073
Both were compiled over long periods, so dating them precisely is not possible.

28
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:03,221
When does the written zero first appear?

29
00:03:03,221 --> 00:03:10,239
The Gwalior inscription of the ninth century has long been the standard answer, because it is firmly dated and unambiguous.

30
00:03:12,433 --> 00:03:18,595
Radiocarbon work on the Bakhshali manuscript has been argued to push a written zero several centuries earlier,

31
00:03:18,595 --> 00:03:23,259
but that manuscript is made of leaves of different ages and the dating is disputed.

32
00:03:23,259 --> 00:03:30,054
Give the Gwalior inscription as the firmly dated case, mention the Bakhshali argument, and say the question is open.

33
00:03:33,967 --> 00:03:36,478
Here is the memory tip, carved in stone.

34
00:03:36,478 --> 00:03:41,677
Zero, surgery, and the spinning earth — three things this tradition worked out.

35
00:03:47,067 --> 00:03:47,798
Dig Quiz.

36
00:03:47,798 --> 00:03:49,189
Pause and name it.

37
00:03:49,189 --> 00:03:55,701
First: the texts on the geometry of altar construction, where Indian mathematics begins.

38
00:03:55,701 --> 00:03:57,392
The Shulba Sutras.

39
00:04:00,267 --> 00:04:07,004
Second: the seventh-century mathematician who set out rules for arithmetic with zero and negative numbers.

40
00:04:07,004 --> 00:04:08,122
Brahmagupta.

41
00:04:11,433 --> 00:04:16,611
Third: the medical compilation describing surgical procedures and instruments.

42
00:04:16,611 --> 00:04:18,053
The Sushruta Samhita.

43
00:04:18,053 --> 00:04:23,465
In Mains, a frequent question asks what ancient India contributed to science.

44
00:04:25,667 --> 00:04:33,467
Be specific and be careful: name the text, the idea and roughly when, and do not claim modern discoveries for ancient texts.

45
00:04:37,333 --> 00:04:40,438
A stone with a circle on it takes its place in the gallery.

46
00:04:48,333 --> 00:04:53,047
Next gallery: the places where this was taught — and what happened to them.

47
00:04:53,047 --> 00:04:56,882
To catch it when it's out, subscribe to Gyaan-X Studios.

